{
 "name": "poly_merge_boundary_divergence",
 "description": "Merging a polygon selection into one face: which boundary the merge walks. Two measured cells. (a) An ANNULUS -- a 3x3 grid of quads with the centre face missing, all 8 faces selected. The reference emits one face whose ring has 18 corners: the walk enters the hole along the edge (2,0,2)-(2,0,3), goes round the hole and leaves along that same edge, so both of those vertices stand in the ring twice; it keeps all 16 vertices and 17 edges. We emit the 12-corner OUTER boundary only -- the hole is paved over, and the 4 vertices that bounded it are deleted, leaving 12 vertices and 12 edges. (b) Two quads that share an edge but are wound OPPOSITELY. The reference merges them into one hexagon (6 vertices, 6 edges, 1 face). We leave both quads standing, shared edge and all (2 faces, 7 edges) -- the vertex set is identical on both sides, so this cell is invisible to any check that compares vertices alone.",
 "source": "Reference side: headless command-port capture of the merge command on the two bases below (dirty-geometry sweep, task 1123, cases merge_all_grid_hole / merge_all_wind_flip). vibe3d side: live drive of this build through the steps stored in each case.",
